\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Hierarchical Gaussians share a site fourth moment}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{Let #{p\ge 2} and #{k\ge 1} be integers, and let the sites be the
residues modulo #{p^k}. Let #{E_j} and #{P_j} be the nested averages
and orthogonal projectors of the [nested blocks](fdip-0099), and let
#{Q=\sum_{j=0}^{k}c_jP_j} with every coefficient #{c_j} positive.
Then #{Q} is a \vocabk{positive precision}{fdip-0004}. Let #{h} be the
counting inner product and let #{\delta_x} be the indicator of a
site. The value
##{\sigma^2=h(\delta_x,Q^{-1}\delta_x)}
does not depend on #{x}. In the counting basis of residue indicators
it equals
##{\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}c_j^{-1}\bigl(p^{-j}-p^{-(j+1)}\bigr)
 +c_k^{-1}p^{-k}.}
The centered \vocabk{Gaussian law}{fdip-0005} of #{Q} gives every site the
second moment #{\sigma^2}, and the fourth moment at each site is
#{3\sigma^4}.}

\p{For distinct sites #{x} and #{y}, let #{v} be the largest integer
such that #{x\equiv y\pmod{p^v}}. Then #{0\le v\le k-1}, and the kernel value
#{h(\delta_x,Q\delta_y)} depends only on #{v}.}

\p{The common value #{3\sigma^4} is the Gaussian moment of one quartic
monomial. \em{No non-Gaussian hierarchical fixed point is proved
here.} The paper of Abdesselam, Chandra and Guadagni,
arXiv:1302.5971, has not been read, and this note does not state
their result. Nothing proved here shows that the nearest-neighbour
path precision, the \vocabk{finite Wilson action}{fdip-006O}, or the
[fourth-power trace](fdip-00A7) is a hierarchical precision on a
residue ring.}

\proof{
\p{Each class of #{E_j} has #{p^j} sites, and #{E_j} replaces every
value by the mean of its class. In the counting basis the matrix
entry is #{p^{-j}} when the two sites are congruent modulo
#{p^{k-j}}, and zero otherwise. The diagonal entry is #{p^{-j}} at
every site. For #{j<k} the diagonal of #{P_j=E_j-E_{j+1}} is
#{p^{-j}-p^{-(j+1)}}, and the diagonal of #{P_k=E_k} is #{p^{-k}}.}

\p{The projectors are symmetric, pairwise orthogonal and sum to the
identity. Symmetry gives #{h(v,P_jv)=h(P_jv,P_jv)}. Expanding
#{v=\sum_i P_iv} and using #{h(P_iv,P_jv)=h(v,P_iP_jv)=0} for
#{i\ne j} gives
##{h(v,v)=\sum_{j=0}^{k}h(P_jv,P_jv).}
The quadratic form is therefore
##{h(v,Qv)=\sum_{j=0}^{k}c_j\,h(P_jv,P_jv).}
Each summand is nonnegative. If #{v\ne 0}, then #{h(v,v)>0}, so
the form is at least the least coefficient times #{h(v,v)} and is
positive. Thus #{Q} is a positive precision. The [nested blocks](fdip-0099) give an inverse
that multiplies the image of #{P_j} by #{c_j^{-1}}, hence
#{Q^{-1}=\sum_{j=0}^{k}c_j^{-1}P_j}. Every diagonal entry equals
#{\sigma^2}. The [inverse-precision theorem](fdip-000B) converts
that entry into the second moment of the coordinate.}

\p{Fix a site #{x} and let #{X} be the line through the counting basis
vector #{\delta_x}. Retaining that line, the
[retained Gaussian marginal](fdip-000E) is the Gaussian law on
#{X} whose precision #{S} satisfies #{h(\delta_x,S^{-1}\delta_x)=\sigma^2}.
The coordinate on #{X} is the scalar #{t} in #{t\delta_x}. Its law
is the scalar Gaussian of precision #{a=1/\sigma^2}.}

\p{Write #{g_a(t)=\ue^{-at^2/2}}. The product rule gives
##{(t^3g_a(t))'=3t^2g_a(t)-at^4g_a(t).}
Integration on #{[-R,R]} gives
##{a\int_{-R}^{R}t^4g_a(t)\,\extd t
 =3\int_{-R}^{R}t^2g_a(t)\,\extd t
 -2R^3\ue^{-aR^2/2}.}
For #{u\ge 0} the exponential series gives #{\ue^{u}\ge u^2/2}. At
#{u=aR^2/2} and #{R\ge 1} the boundary term is at most
#{16/(a^2R)}, so it tends to zero. The integrands are nonnegative,
and [polynomial moments](fdip-002W) make their full integrals finite,
so the compact integrals increase to those limits. Therefore
##{a\int_{\mathbb R}t^4g_a(t)\,\extd t
 =3\int_{\mathbb R}t^2g_a(t)\,\extd t.}
The [scalar Gaussian integral](fdip-002S) is #{I(a)}. Dividing by it
and using the scalar identity #{\int t^2g_a=I(a)/a} from the
[inverse-precision theorem](fdip-000B) gives the normalized moment
#{3/a^2=3\sigma^4}. The polynomial pullback in \ref{fdip-000E}
carries this moment to the coordinate #{\phi(x)}.}

\p{If #{x\ne y}, the integer #{v} above is the largest for which the
two sites are congruent modulo #{p^v}, and #{v\le k-1} because the
sites are distinct residues modulo #{p^k}. Congruence modulo
#{p^{k-j}} holds exactly when #{k-j\le v}. The matrix entry of
#{E_j} at this pair is therefore determined by #{v}. The same is
true of every #{P_j}, and of #{Q}.}
}
